\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $G$ be a group and $K$ a field. If $G$ is torsion-free, must $K[G]$ have no nontrivial units, no nonzero zero divisors, and no idempotents other than $0$ and $1$; and for arbitrary $G$, must $K[G]$ be directly finite?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2022/11}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $G$ be a group and $K$ a field. If $G$ is torsion-free, must $K[G]$ have no nontrivial units, no nonzero zero divisors, and no idempotents other than $0$ and $1$; and for arbitrary $G$, must $K[G]$ be directly finite?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
